<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 9: Putting it into Practice  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  NOTES (see also notes/ch3-4-research.md, which covers this chapter's sources):
  - This chapter is the old "AI in Education: Friend or Foe?" and "Changing
    the Question" chapters, merged. It is the application chapter: the ideas
    of the book put to work in education, and then in your own setting.
  - Moodle assignment: a reflection (200 to 300 words) and a Responsible AI
    Toolkit, in a format of the learner's choice. Both are exercises 1 and 2.
  - Everything David, Lily or Helen say in podcasts 073, 074 and 075 is
    attributed to them. Not verified.
  - The toolkit questions are our own synthesis of the book, a starting
    point and not a standard.
  - Practise (STACK) section removed on purpose. Questions go in the exercises.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-putting-it-into-practice" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Putting it into Practice</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-putting-it-into-practice">
        <p>
            Here's a simple test.
            Take an assignment, paste it into a chatbot, and read what comes
            back.
            If the answer would get a good mark, then the assignment has a
            problem.
        </p>

        <p>
            We've spent eight chapters looking at what AI is, how it goes
            wrong, and who is responsible.
            This chapter is where we use it.
            Education is our worked example, because it's where many readers
            work, and because it's changing fast.
            A technology like the calculator shook up education before, and
            the course notes remind us that exams ended up splitting in two:
            papers where you could use one, and papers where you couldn't
            (<xref ref="biblio-course-notes-2023" text="custom">Course notes, 2023</xref>).
            Generative AI is a bigger shake-up, because it writes.
        </p>

        <p>
            First we ask whether AI in education is a friend or a foe, and
            why David thinks it matters less than you'd think
            (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-073" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 73, n.d.</xref>).
            Then we look at the third way, which is to change the question.
            In the course, the idea is that if a student can complete an
            assignment with generative AI, then the wrong question is being
            asked
            (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-075" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 75, n.d.</xref>).
            And we finish by building a toolkit that you can use in your own
            setting, whether that's a classroom, a clinic, an office or a
            community group.
        </p>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain why detecting AI use is a weak response, and what
                        to do instead.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use evidence, and not only opinion, to judge whether an
                        AI tool helps people to learn.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Rewrite a task so that it asks people to critique,
                        defend or iterate, and not only to produce.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Build a short toolkit, from what you've learned in this
                        book, for using or recommending AI responsibly.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-friend-or-foe-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: Kenya's Universities Decide</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-friend-or-foe-gap">
            <title>A decision that hasn't been made yet</title>

            <p>
                Kenya's 2026 AI policy, which we read in Chapter 7, says
                something direct about education.
                It says that AI isn't systematically integrated into formal
                education curricula, and that teacher capacity in AI remains
                limited.
                It says there is no national AI competency framework that
                covers educators
                (<xref ref="biblio-kenya-policy-2026" text="custom">Republic of Kenya, 2026</xref>,
                section 2.11.4).
                What it proposes is that the Government will provide guidelines
                for readiness and integration across all levels of the
                education system, and support training for teachers
                (section 3.3.2 and 3.3.3).
            </p>

            <p>
                So, for now, a lecturer in Kenya who wants to know what to do
                about AI in their course is mostly left to decide.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-friend-or-foe-workshops">
            <title>A workshop, and then a dialogue</title>

            <p>
                In August 2025, the Commission for University Education (CUE)
                and DAAD, the German academic exchange service, ran a
                three-day workshop at Naivasha on competence-based education
                and AI.
                The Star's report lists the workshop's recommendations: finalise
                AI integration in the national competence-based education
                framework, set up university task forces, sensitise faculty,
                find resources for infrastructure, training and research, and
                form a community of practice
                (<xref ref="biblio-star-cue-2025" text="custom">Okiddy, 2025</xref>).
                It reports recommendations, and doesn't describe an adopted policy.
            </p>

            <p>
                A year later, CUE convened a national dialogue on an AI
                framework for university education, from 18 to 20 August 2026,
                with about 54 universities.
                We only have one university's account of it, from Africa
                International University.
                That account describes a provisional framework with four
                areas, including teaching, learning and assessment, and calls
                the close of the dialogue <q>the beginning of the drafting
                phase</q>
                (<xref ref="biblio-aiu-2026" text="custom">Africa International University, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The same account says that the principles raised included
                human oversight and accountability for decisions that AI
                supports.
                It says that universities shouldn't rely primarily on prohibition
                or AI detectors, and that assessment should show students' thinking,
                through oral defence, projects and reflection.
                And it says that where a university requires students to use a
                particular AI tool, access shouldn't depend exclusively on a student's
                ability to buy it
                (<xref ref="biblio-aiu-2026" text="custom">Africa International University, 2026</xref>).
                As far as we can find, no national framework for universities
                had been adopted when this was written.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    Notice what's happening.
                    The sector is moving towards the conclusion that David
                    reaches in the podcast below: don't build assessment on
                    catching AI use.
                    But the framework isn't finished, and it's a draft.
                    Until it is, each lecturer is making this choice alone.
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-friend-or-foe-evidence">
            <title>What happens when students get AI?</title>

            <p>
                The strongest evidence we found isn't from Kenya.
                Researchers at the University of Pennsylvania and elsewhere ran a
                field experiment with nearly 1,000 high school maths students in
                Turkey
                (<xref ref="biblio-wharton-2024" text="custom">Basiouny, 2024</xref>).
                The students were in one of three groups.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-friend-or-foe-turkey">
                <title>The Turkish maths experiment, as the Wharton write-up reports it</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Group</cell>
                        <cell>What they had in practice</cell>
                        <cell>During practice</cell>
                        <cell>On the exam, without AI</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Control</cell>
                        <cell>Textbook and notes only</cell>
                        <cell>The comparison</cell>
                        <cell>The comparison</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>GPT Base</cell>
                        <cell>A chat interface like ChatGPT-4</cell>
                        <cell>48% better</cell>
                        <cell>17% worse</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>GPT Tutor</cell>
                        <cell>The same, with teacher input, and hints instead of answers</cell>
                        <cell>127% better</cell>
                        <cell>About the same</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                The write-up says that the students with the plain chatbot
                depended on it to solve the problems, and didn't learn the ideas as
                well.
                It also says that students who used AI, even the high achievers,
                were overly optimistic about how much they'd learned.
                The tutor version, designed to give hints, avoided the drop on the
                exam
                (<xref ref="biblio-wharton-2024" text="custom">Basiouny, 2024</xref>).
                The full paper appeared in a journal in June 2025
                (<xref ref="biblio-bastani-2025" text="custom">Bastani and others, 2025</xref>).
                We've only read the Wharton write-up, so we can't tell you the
                exact sizes of the effects, or how they were measured.
                The authors call it a <q>cautionary tale</q>, and note that the
                tools can still hallucinate.
            </p>

            <p>
                It's also a single study, about maths, in one school, in
                Turkey, at a time when chatbots were new.
                But it makes the point well.
                <em>Friend or foe</em> isn't a property of the tool.
                It depends on how the tool is used.
            </p>
        </subsection>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-friend-or-foe-podcast">
        <title>Friend, Foe, or Neither?</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-friend-or-foe-podcast">
            <p>
                This is the third episode in the series that David and I made
                for lecturers.
                In it, David says that he's interested in whether it matters
                at all that AI is a friend or a foe, because we have to deal
                with it either way.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-friend-or-foe-podcast-073">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 73: AI in Education: Friend or Foe?</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-friend-or-foe-podcast-073"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/4213/073-ai-in-education-friend-or-foe.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-friend-or-foe-friend">
            <title>The friend</title>

            <p>
                In the episode I say that I was surprised how many case studies
                there were of lecturers treating AI as a friend.
                One was Emily Donahoe, from the University of Mississippi, who
                has students critique AI-written arguments and then rewrite
                them.
                Another was a group of New York schools that I'd read were
                using AI to help teachers with administrative work, so that
                they'd have more time for creative teaching
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-073" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 73, n.d.</xref>).
                (These are our accounts from 2024, and we haven't rechecked
                them.)
            </p>

            <p>
                David's bigger dream is the private tutor.
                He describes work by Bloom, who compared normal lectures with
                teaching to mastery, where students repeat assessments with
                feedback until they've mastered a concept.
                David says that mastery gets about one standard deviation
                higher, and a good private tutor about two.
                He says that there have been critiques, but that he buys
                the broad idea, which is that personalised teaching gets better
                results.
                If AI could provide that for everyone, he says, the baseline
                education on offer could be much higher, and the educator's job
                would be to build on top.
            </p>

            <p>
                He's clear that it isn't here yet, and he thinks it's years away.
                We look at what is known about AI tutors in Chapter 10.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-friend-or-foe-foe">
            <title>The foe, and the arms race</title>

            <p>
                Most people, David says, think about the foe.
                I mention that Ofqual, the exams regulator in England,
                recommended doing coursework in school, so that it becomes more
                like an exam.
                David says he's not against this.
                He says that if you can't know what people do when you're not
                there, then doing things in person, with a human element, is a
                reasonable response.
            </p>

            <p>
                What he's against is the emphasis on <em>identification</em>.
                I describe what I found when I looked it up.
                Teachers are told to compare a piece of work with the student's
                earlier work, and to look for American spellings and
                overly wordy language.
                On the next page, students are told how to avoid being caught,
                which includes using UK spellings.
                David calls this an arms race that isn't constructive for
                anyone, and says he's confident that the students who learn to
                hide their use will win.
                The losers, I add, are the students who are accused of using
                AI when they haven't.
            </p>

            <p>
                He retells the Reading University study from Chapter 2, where, as
                he tells it, only two of 33 AI-written exam scripts were
                identified.
                He says that in first and second year exams the AI scripts
                outperformed the average student, so students have an incentive
                to use AI
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-073" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 73, n.d.</xref>).
                (As in Chapter 2, this is David's account, and we haven't checked
                the study ourselves.)
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    David's recommendation is a simple one.
                    If you treat AI as a foe, don't try to catch people.
                    Create situations where it can't be used.
                    Oral exams are one example.
                    He says that if you're going to take it out of the
                    equation, then take it out, and don't try to catch the
                    people who use it anyway.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                This links back to Chapter 5, where we saw why detectors fail
                for the people who write in a second language, and in
                particular to the word <q>delve</q>, which is an ordinary word in
                Nigerian English.
                We'll come back to it in Chapter 10, where David puts detection in
                the box he calls <q>never</q>.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-friend-or-foe-third">
            <title>Living with it</title>

            <p>
                David's main point is that it's like a housemate, or your
                family.
                You don't choose them, and whether they're a friend or a foe
                matters less than learning to live with them.
                So the sensible thing is to find positive ways to treat AI as
                a friend, and to recognise that creating AI-free spaces also
                has value.
                He expects both to matter, for almost any lecturer.
            </p>

            <p>
                He imagines that if everyone had an AI tutor, wealthy schools and
                universities would complement it with intense human interaction.
                He gives the very small-group teaching at Oxford and Cambridge as the
                kind of thing that no tutor system would replace.
                So he'd like lecturers to think about how to give students a better
                education, using AI where it helps and building AI-free spaces where
                human interaction matters.
            </p>

            <p>
                He also gives a nightmare scenario.
                Commercial AI tutors are built and used at scale, and the money
                that would have gone to academics goes to AI companies instead.
                Those companies, he says, won't be furthering learning.
                His argument for why lecturers' jobs shouldn't be in danger is
                that AI is built from data from the past, whereas good
                academics create the knowledge of the future.
                It depends, he says, on society recognising what lecturers
                add.
            </p>

            <p>
                One university has turned something like this into a policy.
                The University of Sydney calls it a two-lane approach.
                Lane 1 is secure assessment, in person, which checks that
                students have met the learning outcomes, and which may or may
                not allow AI.
                Lane 2 is open assessment, for learning, where the use of AI is
                scaffolded and supported.
                The authors say that <q>any unenforceable restriction damages
                assessment validity</q>, and that for work that isn't secured, it's
                not possible to detect AI use reliably or equitably
                (<xref ref="biblio-sydney-2024" text="custom">Bridgeman and Liu, 2024</xref>).
                That's close to David's advice.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-changing-the-question-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: The Problem Set That Came Back Perfect</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-changing-the-question-cornell">
            <title>What one lecturer changed</title>

            <p>
                An Associated Press report from April 2026 describes educators
                in the United States who found that written homework was
                coming back perfect, but that students couldn't explain it
                (<xref ref="biblio-ap-orals-2026" text="custom">Gekcer, 2026</xref>).
                One of them is Chris Schaffer, a biomedical engineering
                lecturer at Cornell.
            </p>

            <p>
                He didn't ban AI, and he didn't try to detect it.
                He changed what gets graded.
                Students still submit written problem sets, but teaching
                assistants no longer grade them.
                Instead, each student signs up for a 20-minute discussion in
                which he asks questions about their answers, and that's what is
                graded.
                The article says that his aim is to give students a reason to do
                the work themselves
                (<xref ref="biblio-ap-orals-2026" text="custom">Gekcer, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The question has changed from <q>produce a correct answer</q>
                to <q>show that you understand it</q>.
                A chatbot can produce the first.
                It can't sit in the room for the second.
            </p>

            <p>
                The article describes other versions.
                At the University of Pennsylvania, Emily Hammer pairs oral exams
                with written papers.
                She forbids AI on the writing, but tells her class that she
                knows she can't enforce it.
                At NYU's Stern School, Panos Ipeirotis built an AI-powered oral
                exam, in which a voice asks students about their group project.
                Student feedback was mixed.
                One student said that the conversation felt choppy, with odd pauses,
                and that it was jarring to hear a voice and not see a person
                (<xref ref="biblio-ap-orals-2026" text="custom">Gekcer, 2026</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-changing-the-question-limits">
            <title>What it costs</title>

            <p>
                The article is open about some of the problems.
                Some students find oral exams unsettling, especially if they're
                shy or have serious anxiety, and the person who leads Cornell's
                oral exam training suggests explaining the format beforehand and
                starting with easy questions
                (<xref ref="biblio-ap-orals-2026" text="custom">Gekcer, 2026</xref>).
                It reports no measured results, such as less cheating or better
                learning.
                Time is our own point, and not the article's: a 20-minute
                conversation with each student is a lot of lecturer time, and
                Schaffer's class has 70 students.
                So it's a promising idea that hasn't been tested.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    For a Kenyan lecturer with several hundred students in a
                    lecture hall, 20 minutes each isn't possible.
                    That doesn't make the idea wrong.
                    It means that the question has to be changed in a way that
                    fits your class: a short presentation, a viva on a sample,
                    a quick written explanation done in class, or a peer
                    discussion.
                    What matters is that students stand by their work.
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-changing-the-question-skills">
        <title>Higher-Order Skills</title>

        <p>
            The course uses Bloom's taxonomy, a way of sorting thinking skills
            into levels.
            The lower levels are to <em>remember</em> facts, to
            <em>understand</em> ideas, and to <em>apply</em> information to new
            situations.
            The higher levels are to <em>analyse</em>, which means connecting
            ideas, to <em>evaluate</em>, which means critiquing or justifying
            decisions, and to <em>create</em> original work
            (<xref ref="biblio-course-notes-2023b" text="custom">Course notes, 2023</xref>).
            The taxonomy has critics, who say it's too simple, but the idea
            that there are different kinds of thinking is widely accepted.
        </p>

        <p>
            Generative AI is good at the lower levels, such as grammar,
            summaries and routine documents.
            The course notes argue that this frees us to ask more of students. We
            can spend teaching time on the higher levels, which many courses don't
            cover much.
        </p>

        <p>
            David adds a twist in the episode.
            He says that higher education has put a lot of weight on
            writing: essays, exams, reports.
            He'd like more weight on <em>critical reading</em>.
            In his view, creating something from a blank page isn't the only
            creative act.
            There's a lot of creativity in improving something, spotting what's
            missing, and adding value to it.
            When he takes on graduates, he says, the first tasks he gives
            them are to adapt and critique, because that's what adds value
            at work
            (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-075" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 75, n.d.</xref>).
        </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-changing-the-question-podcasts">
        <title>Changing the Question in Practice</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-changing-the-question-podcasts">
            <p>
                Two episodes look at how.
                In the first, David and I go through the idea and some
                case studies.
                In the second, a bonus episode, David talks to Helen Crompton, a
                researcher and lecturer in educational technology.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-changing-the-question-podcast-075">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 75: Changing The Question 2</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-changing-the-question-podcast-075"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/4235/075-changing-the-question-2.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-changing-the-question-podcast-074">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 74: Changing the Question (with Helen Crompton)</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-changing-the-question-podcast-074"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/4218/074-changing-the-question.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-changing-the-question-critique">
            <title>Critique</title>

            <p>
                One example that David likes is to give the essay topic to a
                chatbot, and give the students its answer to critique.
                They have to find what needs fact-checking, what is wrong
                with the language, and what's worth digging into.
                He says that you can assess this, for example with track
                changes and comments, and that it tests whether a student
                understands the text
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-075" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 75, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Helen describes how she does it with students.
                They choose a topic, have an AI tool write a paper on it, and put
                it in a Word document.
                Then they critique it in track changes and comments, and also
                ask whether there's an alternative way of thinking about the
                topic.
                She says that this reaches the highest level of Bloom's
                taxonomy, because to critique well you have to understand
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-074" text="custom">Stern and Crompton, episode 74, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-changing-the-question-debate-interview">
            <title>Debate and interview</title>

            <p>
                Helen's other two ideas are the debate and the interview.
                She says that all three come from an OpenAI guide for educators,
                and that she uses them in her own classes.
                In a debate, students choose a topic, check it with her, and debate
                it, and can go back to the readings if they need to.
                She starts with something silly, like the best flavour of ice
                cream, and then moves on to topics like whether fluoride should
                be in drinking water, for her dentistry students.
                In an interview, an AI plays the interviewer for a job, and asks
                one question at a time, responding to each answer
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-074" text="custom">Stern and Crompton, episode 74, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                David's comments are that the interview gives feedback that
                wouldn't be possible at scale without AI, and that a debate is a
                setting where AI can't really do the work for you.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-changing-the-question-ownership">
            <title>Ownership</title>

            <p>
                David's strongest point is about ownership.
                He says that he doesn't mind whether a thought was generated
                by AI or written by the student, as long as the student will
                stand by it.
                So he'd rather have a one-page paragraph followed by an oral
                defence than a long essay.
                He thinks that students could end up with more ownership of what
                they hand in with AI than before, and calls it a
                <q>beautiful irony</q> that artificial intelligence might make
                human intelligence more important
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-075" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 75, n.d.</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                I add some other examples from the course.
                A bioscience professor assesses students on their prompts, as
                well as their work, because a small change in a prompt can
                produce a very different answer.
                I also talk about generating images, where asking for someone
                <q>black</q> gave me a silhouette.
                David says that the details of how to ask will change, but that
                noticing how changing the question changes the answer is a
                lasting skill.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-changing-the-question-diversity">
            <title>The risk: everyone's work looks the same</title>

            <p>
                There's a catch, and the episodes are open about it.
                I describe a study that I read, in which two groups wrote
                stories, and one group got ideas from AI.
                The AI-assisted stories were enjoyed more by readers, because they had
                features like plot twists, but they were less varied
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-075" text="custom">Clements and Stern, episode 75, n.d.</xref>).
                (We haven't identified or checked the study here.)
            </p>

            <p>
                David links this to Chapter 2.
                AI finds what is common and normal, so if we're not careful it
                reduces diversity.
                He says that lecturers now have a chance to reward students who
                know the popular choice and make a different one on purpose.
                His example is someone who deliberately leaves out the plot
                twist that the AI suggests.
                Helen, in the other episode, says that she's seen the systems
                get more diverse, and gives the example of a pupil whose language,
                Tagalog, wasn't recognised by a tool at first, but which she says
                is now supported
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-074" text="custom">Stern and Crompton, episode 74, n.d.</xref>).
                So she's more optimistic than David about this.
            </p>

            <p>
                Helen also raises a different worry.
                If AI ever became perfect, and never made a mistake, would we stop
                checking?
                She says that students are taught that something on the
                internet isn't automatically real, and that AI output should be
                critically reviewed too.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-changing-the-question-ideas">
        <title>More Ways to Change the Question</title>

        <p>
            The course first collected examples from teachers in 2023 and
            2024, and they've moved on a lot since.
            Here are some that we've read about more recently.
        </p>


        <table xml:id="table-changing-the-question-newer">
            <title>Examples</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Idea</cell>
                    <cell>What changed</cell>
                    <cell>Source</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Oral defence of written work</cell>
                    <cell>Cornell: students defend problem sets in 20 minutes, and that is what's graded.</cell>
                    <cell>AP, April 2026</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>An AI that examines you</cell>
                    <cell>NYU Stern: a voice asks students about their group project. Feedback was mixed.</cell>
                    <cell>AP, April 2026</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>A tutor that gives hints, not answers</cell>
                    <cell>In the Turkish maths trial, the version built to give hints avoided the exam drop that the plain chatbot caused.</cell>
                    <cell>Wharton, 2024</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Two kinds of assessment</cell>
                    <cell>Sydney: secure, in-person tasks for attainment, and open tasks where AI is scaffolded, for learning.</cell>
                    <cell>University of Sydney</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Assessing thinking</cell>
                    <cell>Kenya's draft university framework: oral defence, projects and reflection, and not mainly prohibition or detection.</cell>
                    <cell>AIU, 2026</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The Turkish study is a useful warning for this list.
            A change that looks like using AI to learn can have the opposite
            effect, if students use it to skip the thinking.
            So each idea needs a test: does the student still have to do the
            thinking?
        </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-changing-the-question-worked-example">
        <title>Try It: Change One Question</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-changing-the-question-worked-example">
            <p>
                Here's an example that we made up, using what we learned in
                Chapter 4.
                The original question is: <q>Explain why Kenya's SHA means test
                has been criticised.</q>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-changing-the-question-example">
            <title>Four versions</title>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>The original.</em>
                        A chatbot can write a plausible answer in seconds.
                        It's a test of whether you can recall and summarise.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Critique.</em>
                        Here is a chatbot's answer.
                        Using Chapter 4, find two claims that you can check, and
                        say whether each is supported.
                        What did it leave out?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Defend.</em>
                        Write one paragraph on whether the means test should
                        be kept, changed or dropped.
                        Be ready to defend it in a two-minute conversation.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>Iterate.</em>
                        Ask a chatbot for a better means test.
                        Then say what it got wrong, what you'd change, and who
                        would be affected by the change.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
                The first is lower-order.
                The other three are about analysing and evaluating.
                None of them is AI-proof.
                But each one asks the student to be responsible for the answer, and a
                chatbot can't do that for them.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-changing-the-question-yours">
            <title>Now you</title>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Pick one question that you've set, or been set.
                        Paste it into a chatbot and read the answer.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Which level of Bloom's taxonomy is it asking for?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Could you make it secure, so that a student can't use
                        AI?
                        If not, a rule against AI can't be enforced, and
                        it belongs in the open lane.
                        If you can, what would it cost you?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Rewrite it to critique, to defend and to
                        iterate.
                        Which one fits your subject, and your class size,
                        best?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Test your new question.
                        Would a student who just pasted it into a chatbot get
                        a good mark?
                        If so, change it again.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- Toolkit                                                        -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-putting-it-into-practice-toolkit">
        <title>Build Your Toolkit</title>

        <introduction xml:id="intro-putting-it-into-practice-toolkit">
            <p>
                The last part of the course asks you to make something that you
                could keep or share, in your community, workplace or
                institution.
                It could be a checklist, a set of questions, a one-page guide
                or a personal code of conduct.
                We think the most useful way to start is to take one question
                from each chapter.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-putting-it-into-practice-questions">
            <title>One question from each chapter</title>

            <p>
                Here's a first set.
                It's our own summary of the book, so change it to fit your
                setting.
                Think of it as the questions to ask before you use, buy or
                recommend an AI tool.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-putting-it-into-practice-questions">
                <title>A starting set of questions</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Chapter</cell>
                        <cell>Question to ask</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>1. Introduction</cell>
                        <cell>Is there a person in the loop, who can change the outcome?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>2. Demystifying AI</cell>
                        <cell>What data was it trained on, and who labelled it?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>3. Illusion of Autonomy</cell>
                        <cell>Is it AI doing the work, or people we can't see? Does the company say so?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>4. Misuse and Misinterpretation</cell>
                        <cell>What does the score really measure? What happens when it's wrong, and can someone appeal?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>5. AI and Bias</cell>
                        <cell>Who was left out of the data, and has it been tested in our languages and with our people?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>6. Where does Bias come from?</cell>
                        <cell>What is standing in for what we care about? What happens to people the data doesn't show?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>7. Core Principles</cell>
                        <cell>Which of Kenya's principles does it meet, and who checks?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>8. Who is Responsible?</cell>
                        <cell>Who pays: in labour, energy, water or misinformation? Who answers when it goes wrong?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>9. Putting it into Practice</cell>
                        <cell>Does the user still have to do the thinking? Where would we use it, and where would we keep it out?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>10. Looking to the Future</cell>
                        <cell>Is the claim a Never, a When, an If or an Unknown, and what would change that?</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-putting-it-into-practice-format">
            <title>Pick a format</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A checklist for evaluating AI tools.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A set of questions that you'd ask before using or
                        recommending an AI product.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A one-page guide or infographic for your workplace,
                        school or community.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A personal code of conduct for how you'll engage with AI
                        tools.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                Whichever you choose, test it on a real tool before you finish.
                If it can't tell a good tool from a bad one, it needs better
                questions.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- Exercises                                                      -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-putting-it-into-practice">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-1">
            <title>Assignment, part A: reflection</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In 200 to 300 words, respond to these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What does responsible AI mean to you, in your
                            context?
                            If you had to explain it to a friend, a colleague
                            or a policymaker in three sentences, what would you
                            say?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What has changed in how you think about AI since the
                            start of the course?
                            (You may want to look back at your first reflection in the course.)
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What is one thing that you'll do differently
                            because of this course?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-2">
            <title>Assignment, part B: your toolkit</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Create a short resource that you could keep or share in your
                    community, workplace or institution.
                    Use the starting questions in this chapter, and choose one
                    format: a checklist, a set of questions, a one-page guide or
                    infographic, or a personal code of conduct.
                    You can make it written or visual, or as slides.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Optional bonus.</em>
                    Add a short statement about how you might share or adapt it
                    in future, for example in a lesson, a meeting, a training or
                    a policy document.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-3">
            <title>Why does the design matter?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the Turkish study, the two groups that used AI did better
                    in practice.
                    Only one did as well on the exam.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Why do you think the first group did worse?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Why might a student prefer the tool that doesn't
                            help them to learn?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you want to know before using this
                            result to make a decision in a Kenyan school?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-4">
            <title>A rule for your university</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Imagine you're on a task force at a Kenyan university, and
                    have to write a one-page rule on AI and assessment.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Would you try to ban AI, detect it or design around
                            it?
                            Why?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you say to a student who is accused of
                            using AI, and says that they didn't?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            How would you make sure that students who can't
                            afford premium AI tools aren't left behind?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-5">
            <title>Where would the money go?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    David's nightmare is that AI tutors are commercial, and
                    the money that went to lecturers goes to AI companies.
                    Is this a risk in Kenya?
                    Who would gain, who would lose, and what could a university,
                    or the government, do about it?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-6">
            <title>Embrace it, or keep it out</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In some situations you may want to embrace AI, and in
                    others you may want to create spaces free of AI.
                    We don't yet fully understand the long-term impact of AI in
                    education.
                    Will it lead to an absence of fundamental skills, or will it
                    cultivate more relevant skills like critical thinking?
                    Share experiences and strategies for changing the question
                    in your context.
                    Where would you embrace it, and where would you keep it
                    out, and why?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-7">
            <title>Ideation, iteration and polishing</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the course, we saw three tasks that you can use a chatbot
                    for: ideation, iteration and polishing.
                    How could you change a question in your subject for each
                    of these, for text, code or media?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-8">
            <title>When the class is huge</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Cornell's oral defence works for 70 students.
                    Think about a class of several hundred.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Which parts of the idea could you keep, and which
                            would you have to give up?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What could students do for each other, such as
                            peer review or debate?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you do about students who find oral
                            assessment very stressful?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-9">
            <title>Sameness</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    I described a study in which AI-assisted stories were
                    enjoyed more but were less varied.
                    If every student in a class uses a chatbot, what happens
                    to the variety of their work?
                    How might you reward a student for making a different
                    choice from the one that the AI suggests?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-putting-it-into-practice-10">
            <title>Is a changed question fair?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Changing the question can create new unfairness.
                    Think about students who can't afford a paid AI tool, who
                    have poor internet, or who are less confident speaking.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Who might be disadvantaged by a critique task, an
                            oral defence or a debate?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would you do to make each one fairer?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

